mint-linux/</link><pubDate>Tue, 07 Jul 2009 11:51:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/google-chrome-on-mint-linux/</guid><description>&lt;div style="float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3697745906/" title="photo sharing"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm3.static.flickr.com/2605/3697745906_ed5976d1e2_m.jpg" alt="" style="border: solid 2px #000000;" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;span style=" margin-top: 0px;font-size:0.9em;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3697745906/"&gt;Google Chrome on Mint Linux&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Originally uploaded by &lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/people/doodle_m/"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Testing the Dev build of Chrome running on my Mint Linux VM.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Feels pretty nippy, especially scrolling through Google Reader. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3697745990/" title="Google Chrome on Mint Linux by Alastair Montgomery, on Flickr"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm3.static.flickr.com/2498/3697745990_9387b939fb.jpg" width="500" height="345" alt="Google Chrome on Mint Linux" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</description></item><item><title>Opera 10 Beta</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/opera-10-beta/</link><pubDate>Thu, 04 Jun 2009 09:06:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/opera-10-beta/</guid><description>&lt;div style="float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3594913138/" title="photo sharing"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm3.static.flickr.com/2481/3594913138_84e9ea9db5_m.jpg" alt="" style="border: solid 2px #000000;" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;span style=" margin-top: 0px;font-size:0.9em;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3594913138/"&gt;Opera 10 Beta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Originally uploaded by &lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/people/doodle_m/"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Been trying out Opera 10 Beta on my Mac.&lt;br /&gt;So far I'm impressed with it, so much I've made it the default browser for now.&lt;br /&gt;It kick the Safari 4 beta off this spot.</description></item><item><title>Find Images by Exact Dimensions, Make Wallpaper Search a Breeze [Google School]</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/find-images-by-exact-dimensions-make/</link><pubDate>Thu, 29 Jan 2009 10:53:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/find-images-by-exact-dimensions-make/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 6 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 11 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Just need to devise a Python script to automate this for Windows now :)&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div style="margin: 0px 2px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: rgb(195, 217, 255); font-size: 1px ! important; line-height: 0px ! important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 1px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: rgb(195, 217, 255); font-size: 1px ! important; line-height: 0px ! important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="padding: 4px; background-color: rgb(195, 217, 255);"&gt;&lt;h3 style="margin: 0px 3px; font-family: sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 1px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: rgb(195, 217, 255); font-size: 1px ! important; line-height: 0px ! important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 2px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: rgb(195, 217, 255); font-size: 1px ! important; line-height: 0px ! important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 10px; overflow: auto; font-family: sans-serif; width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;h2 style="margin: 0.25em 0pt 0pt;"&gt;&lt;div class=""&gt;&lt;a href="http://feeds.gawker.com/%7Er/lifehacker/full/%7E3/l9pRzfSniPU/find-images-by-exact-dimensions-make-wallpaper-search-a-breeze"&gt;Find Images by Exact Dimensions, Make Wallpaper Search a Breeze [Google School]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;div style="margin-bottom: 0.5em;"&gt;via &lt;a href="http://lifehacker.com/" class="f"&gt;Lifehacker&lt;/a&gt; by Adam Pash on 28/01/09&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="http://cache.gawker.com/assets/images/lifehacker/2009/01/cities-wallpaper.png" style="display: block;" height="304" width="504" /&gt;Everyone knows that Google Image Search sports a drop-down menu for filtering results by small, medium, large, and extra large sizes, but if you&amp;rsquo;re looking for the perfect wallpaper, for example, the &lt;code&gt;imagesize&lt;/code&gt; operator is even better.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>OperaTor Browses with Portable, Tor-Driven Anonymity</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/operator-browses-with-portable-tor/</link><pubDate>Wed, 28 Jan 2009 12:30:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/operator-browses-with-portable-tor/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 4 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 7 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Time to dust of that USB stick and browse in the shadows&amp;hellip;&amp;hellip;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div style="margin: 0px 2px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: #c3d9ff; font-size: 1px !important; line-height: 0px !important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 1px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: #c3d9ff; font-size: 1px !important; line-height: 0px !important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="padding: 4px; background-color: #c3d9ff;"&gt;&lt;h3 style="margin:0px 3px;font-family:sans-serif"&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 1px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: #c3d9ff; font-size: 1px !important; line-height: 0px !important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="margin: 0px 2px; padding-top: 1px; background-color: #c3d9ff; font-size: 1px !important; line-height: 0px !important;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div style="font-family:sans-serif;overflow:auto;width:100%;margin: 0px 10px"&gt;&lt;h2 style="margin: 0.25em 0 0 0"&gt;&lt;div class=""&gt;&lt;a href="http://feeds.gawker.com/~r/lifehacker/full/~3/xBxT5jch-g0/operator-browses-with-portable-tor+driven-anonymity"&gt;OperaTor Browses with Portable, Tor-Driven Anonymity [Featured Windows Download]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;div style="margin-bottom: 0.5em"&gt;via &lt;a href="http://lifehacker.com/" class="f"&gt;Lifehacker&lt;/a&gt; by Jason Fitzpatrick on 23/01/09&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="http://cache.gawker.com/assets/images/lifehacker/2009/01/2009-01-23_023214.jpg" align="left" hspace="4" vspace="2" width="612" height="327" style="display:block" /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Windows only: OperaTor combines a thumb-drive-friendly version of the peedy Opera web-browser with transparent privacy through the Tor network.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Lunascape Is Firefox, Chrome, and Internet Explorer Rolled Into One [Featured Windows Early Adopter Download]</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/bloglines-lunascape-is-firefox-chrome/</link><pubDate>Tue, 25 Nov 2008 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/bloglines-lunascape-is-firefox-chrome/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 19 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 20 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.bloglines.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Going on my download list. &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;table id="table9477167" bg="" style="color: rgb(189, 224, 237);" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td rowspan="2"&gt; &lt;table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;span class="blogtitle"&gt;&lt;a href="http://pipes.yahoo.com/pipes/pipe.info?_id=8IsQFi282xG5FaTJJjBjOg"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Life GH Com&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Pipes Output &lt;/td&gt; &lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;div id="items9477167" style="display: block;"&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr bgcolor="#ffffff"&gt; &lt;td&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;a title="Site: Life GH Com" href="http://feeds.gawker.com/%7Er/lifehacker/full/%7E3/RosUatVMjEY/lunascape-is-firefox-chrome-and-internet-explorer-rolled-into-one"&gt;Lunascape Is Firefox, Chrome, and Internet Explorer Rolled Into One [Featured Windows Early Adopter Download]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Faster browsing with RAM disks</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/faster-browsing-with-ram-disks/</link><pubDate>Mon, 24 Nov 2008 20:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/faster-browsing-with-ram-disks/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 4 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 19 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.bloglines.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;table id="table9477167" bg cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" width="100%" style="color:#bde0ed;"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td rowspan="2"&gt; &lt;table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;span class="blogtitle"&gt;&lt;a href="http://pipes.yahoo.com/pipes/pipe.info?_id=8IsQFi282xG5FaTJJjBjOg"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Life GH Com&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Pipes Output &lt;/td&gt; &lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;div id="items9477167" style="display: block;"&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr bgcolor="#ffffff"&gt; &lt;td&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;a title="Site: Life GH Com" href="http://hackaday.com/2008/11/20/faster-browsing-with-ram-disks/"&gt;Faster browsing with RAM disks&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;div class="snap_preview"&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img class="alignnone size-full wp-image-6144" title="esperancedv" src="http://hackadaycom.files.wordpress.com/2008/11/esperancedv.jpg?w=450&amp;amp;h=138" alt="esperancedv" width="450" height="138" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A coworker approached us today wondering if they could get a performance boost using &lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://www.obsessable.com/storage/samsung-256gb-ssd/"&gt;Samsung&amp;rsquo;s newly announced 256GB SSD&lt;/a&gt;. Most of their work is done in browser, so we said &amp;ldquo;no&amp;rdquo;. They&amp;rsquo;d only see benefit if they were reading/writing large files. Their system has plenty of RAM, and we decided to take a different approach. By creating a filesystem in RAM, you can read and write files much faster than on a typical hard drive. We decided to put the browser&amp;rsquo;s file cache into RAM.&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Opera 9.6 released</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/opera-96-released/</link><pubDate>Thu, 09 Oct 2008 11:04:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/opera-96-released/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 24 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 27 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.bloglines.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;My browser of choice for my aging home laptop and desktop computers. &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base2228392"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;table id="table2228392" bgcolor="#bde0ed" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td rowspan="2"&gt; &lt;table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td align="left" valign="top"&gt; &lt;a href="http://www.downloadsquad.com/"&gt;&lt;img src="http://www.downloadsquad.com/media/feedlogo.gif" alt="DownloadSquad" border="0" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;span class="blogtitle"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.downloadsquad.com/"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Download Squad&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Download Squad &lt;/td&gt; &lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;a name="base2228392"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;div id="items2228392" style="display: block;"&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr bgcolor="#ffffff"&gt; &lt;td&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;a title="Site: Download Squad" href="http://feeds.feedburner.com/%7Er/weblogsinc/downloadsquad/%7E3/414976209/"&gt;Opera 9.6 released&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; By Brad Linder on opera-browser &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Firefox Universal Uploader Is Like an FTP Client for Popular Web Sites [Featured Firefox Extension]</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/firefox-universal-uploader-is-like-ftp/</link><pubDate>Tue, 30 Sep 2008 08:55:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/firefox-universal-uploader-is-like-ftp/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 18 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 19 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.bloglines.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Another Firefox addon to add to my growing collection. &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;table id="table9477167" bg cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" width="100%" style="color:#bde0ed;"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td rowspan="2"&gt; &lt;table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;span class="blogtitle"&gt;&lt;a href="http://pipes.yahoo.com/pipes/pipe.info?_id=8IsQFi282xG5FaTJJjBjOg"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Life GH Com&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Pipes Output &lt;/td&gt; &lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;div id="items9477167" style="display: block;"&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr bgcolor="#ffffff"&gt; &lt;td&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;a title="Site: Life GH Com" href="http://feeds.gawker.com/%7Er/lifehacker/full/%7E3/406502645/firefox-universal-uploader-is-like-an-ftp-client-for-popular-web-sites"&gt;Firefox Universal Uploader Is Like an FTP Client for Popular Web Sites [Featured Firefox Extension]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>